\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Corollary}
\title{The oriented exponent on the opposite axial block}
\date{2026-11-26}

\p{On free fields of \vocabk{graded total inversion}{fdip-019C},
take the non-self-opposite spatial axial label #{r} of
\ref{fdip-0177}. Use distinct time, axial and two transverse
coordinates, hence #{n\ge4}, with equal periods on the
transverse pair #{i,j}. For
#{u_{ij}=(\bbI+\varepsilon_i\varepsilon_j)/\sqrt{2}},
choose #{u_{ij}} as the generator at #{r} and #{u_{ij}^{-1}}
at #{-r}, with the same primitive eighth root #{\zeta}.
Total inversion carries the degree #{\nu} subspace onto the
degree #{-\nu} subspace for these two chosen generators.}

\proof{
\p{A field in block #{r} has the form
#{x\mapsto\alpha_r(x)v}, where #{\alpha_r} is the
translation character. Evaluation at #{-x} changes it to
#{\alpha_{-r}(x)}. The spin grading is linear and commutes
with #{\varepsilon_i\varepsilon_j}, since it anticommutes with
both factors. It therefore commutes with the turn #{u_{ij}}.
If the source turn has eigenvalue #{\zeta^\nu} on the
value, the inverse target turn has eigenvalue #{\zeta^{-\nu}}
on the image. The map is invertible and the same calculation
reverses it, giving equality of the indicated subspaces.}

\p{For a connection, a restricting coupled operator needs
the translation compatibility of \ref{fdip-016L}. Using
the turn as a symmetry of such an operator also requires the
actual rotation intertwiner for that background. For other
choices of cyclic generator the degree is given by its
transport as in \ref{fdip-0175}.}
}
